/// \defgroup grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers INI flag-list, label, random-range, draw-group and terrain parsers
/// \ingroup grp_ini
/// \brief Field parsers for damage, death and veterancy flag lists, localised
///        text labels and random ranges, and the block parsers for
///        DrawGroupInfo, Terrain and WaterTextureList.
///
/// These are the parse functions an INI author reaches by writing a field
/// line or a block keyword. \ref INI explains the two signatures: a field
/// parser has four arguments and is called from a FieldParse table when a
/// field name is read; a block parser has only the reader and is called when
/// a line starts with its registered keyword. \ref sub_ini_flow describes how
/// a load drives them.
///
/// \par Field parsers in this group
/// The last column lists fields that use the parser, read from the
/// FieldParse tables in the retail executable. The lists say "includes"
/// because a table can be added to a module or block without this page
/// changing.
/// | Parser | Value it reads | Fields that use it (includes) |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | INI::parseDamageTypeFlags | a list of damage type names | DamageTypes (FireWeaponWhenDamagedBehaviorModuleData, CallHelpOnDamageModuleData), DamageTypesToReflect (ReflectDamageModuleData), PropagateDamageTypesToSlavesWhenExisting (SpawnBehaviorModuleData), DamageFXTypes (BoneFXUpdateModuleData, TransitionDamageFXModuleData, StructureToppleUpdateModuleData), DamageOCLTypes and DamageParticleTypes (BoneFXUpdateModuleData, TransitionDamageFXModuleData) |
/// | INI::parseDeathTypeFlags | a list of death type names | DeathTypes, in the die-condition table shared by the die modules |
/// | INI::parseVeterancyLevelFlags | a list of veterancy level names | no field found in BFME 2 (see its page) |
/// | INI::parseAndTranslateLabel | one string-table label, stored as its translated text | RankName (Rank block), DisplayName and Description (Science block), DisplayName and Description (CommandMap and DebugCommandMap blocks) |
/// | INI::parseGameClientRandomVariable | a low value, a high value and an optional distribution name | particle FX nugget fields such as Radius, Height and InitialDelay, many FXParticleSystem module fields, the ParticleCursor fields of GameData, Weather's LightningFactor, and the cloud-effect and fire definitions' lightning and scorch fields |
/// | parseInt and parsePercentToReal (free functions in INIDrawGroupInfo.cpp) | a pixel or percentage position | the DrawPosition fields of DrawGroupInfo |
///
/// \par Block parsers in this group
/// | Block keyword | Parser | What it fills | Fields |
/// |---|---|---|---|
/// | DrawGroupInfo | INI::parseDrawGroupNumberDefinition | the one DrawGroupInfo record (how the control-group number over a unit is drawn) | UsePlayerColor, ColorForText, ColorForTextDropShadow, FontName, FontSize, FontIsBold, DropShadowOffsetX, DropShadowOffsetY, DrawPositionXPixel, DrawPositionXPercent, DrawPositionYPixel, DrawPositionYPercent |
/// | Terrain | INI::parseTerrainDefinition | one terrain type in the terrain type collection | Texture, BlendEdges, Class, RestrictConstruction, TerrainObject |
/// | WaterTextureList | iniParseWaterTexturesDefinition | one of six named water texture lists | Texture |
///
/// The field names are the names in the retail executable's FieldParse
/// tables. The project's field table listing (`reverse/fieldparse_tables.tsv`,
/// produced by `tools/fieldparse_tables.py`) shows the tables of other blocks.
///
/// \par Names come from fixed tables
/// The flag-list and distribution parsers, and the Class field of a Terrain
/// block, look their names up in lists compiled into the executable, so an
/// INI file can choose among the names but cannot add one: the damage table
/// has 27 names, the death table 24, the veterancy table 4, the distribution
/// table 6 and the terrain class table 47. Each parser's page lists its
/// names. A new damage type, for example, needs more than a change to the
/// flag parser's table. The armor parser scans its own copy of the same 27
/// names and stores 27 coefficients (\ref page_core_frameworks_weapons).
/// Other per-damage-type data is not enumerated here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: every field in the tables above, the block names of
///   Terrain (a terrain type's name, which maps refer to), and the choice of
///   names within each fixed table.
/// - Hard-coded: the block keywords, the field names (case-sensitive), the
///   name tables, the six water texture list names, and the shape of every
///   value (for example a random range is exactly low, high and an optional
///   distribution).
/// - The damage and death flag-list fields all sit in logic-side module
///   data, but they do not all change the simulation. DamageTypes
///   (FireWeaponWhenDamagedBehaviorModuleData, CallHelpOnDamageModuleData),
///   DamageTypesToReflect, DeathTypes (a die module is not applicable when
///   the death type's bit is clear) and DamageOCLTypes (BoneFXUpdate starts
///   an ObjectCreationList, which can create objects, unless the object's
///   last recorded damage has a type whose bit is clear; with no recorded
///   damage the mask does not block it) select what a module does to game
///   state, so every machine in a game must use identical files for them
///   (\ref page_modding_rules, \ref sub_save_load_crc). What
///   PropagateDamageTypesToSlavesWhenExisting gates at run time is not
///   established here; treat it like those fields. DamageFXTypes and
///   DamageParticleTypes only gate effects in the BoneFXUpdate and
///   StructureToppleUpdate code read for this page (an effect list or a
///   particle system is started unless the object's last recorded damage has
///   a type whose bit is clear; with no recorded damage the mask does not
///   block it). They sit in the same logic-side module blocks, so keeping
///   every machine's files identical is still the safe rule.
/// - Random-range fields are sampled from the client random stream, which is
///   not part of the lockstep simulation. Whether an edit to them reaches the
///   INI CRC depends on how their file is loaded. Zero Hour: the INI CRC
///   covers the text of each line read through the CRC writer. BFME 2's line
///   reader makes a call of the same shape, so files loaded through the
///   writer (\ref sub_ini_modding lists which) are very likely covered, but
///   what that call hashes is not established here.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The flag-list masks are plain 32-bit integers, and the code that sets
///   and tests them shifts by the name's position minus one, relying on the
///   x86 rule that a shift count is taken modulo 32 (see
///   INI::parseDamageTypeFlags).
/// - Text labels are stored as 16-bit wide strings, copied out of the string
///   table when the field is read (see INI::parseAndTranslateLabel).
/// - The DrawGroupInfo position fields come in a pixel form, which is added
///   to the anchor in raw screen pixels, and a percentage form that scales
///   with the anchor rectangle (see INI::parseDrawGroupNumberDefinition).
///
/// \see INI, \ref sub_ini, \ref sub_ini_modding

/// \file INIDrawGroupInfo.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers
/// \brief The DrawGroupInfo block parser, its field table and the two
///        position field parsers.
///
/// \see INI::parseDrawGroupNumberDefinition, parseInt, parsePercentToReal

/// \file INITerrain.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers
/// \brief The Terrain and WaterTextureList block parsers and the terrain type
///        field table.
///
/// \see INI::parseTerrainDefinition, iniParseWaterTexturesDefinition

/// \fn static void INI::parseDamageTypeFlags(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers
/// \brief Field parser: reads a list of damage type names, written with ALL,
///        NONE, +NAME and -NAME, into a 32-bit mask.
///
/// Module data uses this for fields that say which damage types a module
/// reacts to. BoneFXUpdateModuleData and StructureToppleUpdateModuleData, for
/// example, test the damage type of the object's last recorded damage (the
/// type minus one, as a shift) against the mask, and skip their effect when
/// that bit is clear. With no recorded damage they do not skip it. The
/// \ref grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers table lists the fields that
/// use it.
///
/// \par Syntax
/// The tokens are split at spaces, tabs and equals signs (the reader's
/// default separators). At least one token is required. They apply left to
/// right to a mask that starts with every bit set:
/// - ALL sets every bit and NONE clears every bit. Both are matched without
///   regard to case.
/// - +NAME sets the bit of one damage type and -NAME clears it.
/// - Any other token stops the load with the message "ALL, NONE, + or -
///   expected".
///
/// Because the mask starts full, a list that only adds names changes
/// nothing: `DamageTypes = +FLAME` still means every damage type. To select
/// types write `NONE +FLAME +SIEGE`; to exclude them write `ALL -HEALING`.
///
/// \par Names and bits
/// NAME is looked up without regard to case, by INI::scanIndexList, in a
/// table of 27 names compiled into the executable: FORCE, CRUSH, SLASH,
/// PIERCE, SIEGE, STRUCTURAL, FLAME, HEALING, UNRESISTABLE, WATER, PENALTY,
/// FALLING, TOPPLING, REFLECTED, PASSENGER, MAGIC, CHOP, HERO, SPECIALIST,
/// URUK, HERO_RANGED, FLY_INTO, UNDEFINED, LOGICAL_FIRE, CAVALRY,
/// CAVALRY_RANGED and POISON. An unknown name stops the load with a message of the form
/// "Token 'NAME' is not a valid member of the index list", where NAME is the
/// name without its sign. The table lists the same names in the same
/// order as the one the armor parser uses, but it is a separate copy.
///
/// The name at position n of the table sets bit n-1, so CRUSH is bit 0 and
/// POISON is bit 25. FORCE is position 0, so the shift count is minus one,
/// and the x86 shift instruction reduces the count modulo 32: FORCE is
/// bit 31. The 27 names therefore get 27 different bits. The code that tests
/// a mask uses the same expression.
///
/// The mask is written to the field with one store after the last token, so
/// an error leaves the field as it was (the load stops anyway).
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The 32-bit mask field.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \note Zero Hour: the body is the same loop, but the mask is a BitFlags set
///       sized for Zero Hour's own damage types (\ref page_core_frameworks_weapons
///       gives both counts), the bit comes from DamageTypeFlags::getSingleBitFromName,
///       and a bad token throws without message text. BFME 2's 32-bit mask and
///       its message text are from the BFME 2 body.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which damage types a field covers, from the 27 names.
///   A mod cannot add a damage type here: the name table is in the
///   executable.
/// - The fields that use this parser sit in logic-side module data. Most of
///   them select what a module does to game state and so must be identical
///   on every machine in a game; DamageFXTypes and DamageParticleTypes only
///   gate effects (\ref grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers lists which
///   fields are which, and \ref page_modding_rules covers the lockstep rule).
/// - Remember that a list of only +NAME entries means all damage types.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The mask and its tests rely on a shift by a negative count (position
/// minus one for the first name). That is undefined in C++ and works on x86
/// only because the hardware wraps the count. A port to another compiler or
/// CPU has to keep the mapping (first name is bit 31) in both this parser and
/// every place that tests a mask, or the two will disagree.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Facts from BFME 2 evidence: the retail FieldParse tables reference this
/// function from the fields above; the 27 names, the two message texts and
/// the shift by position minus one are read from the retail data and body.
/// The loop and the ALL/NONE/+/- syntax are Zero Hour's parseDamageTypeFlags
/// (INI.cpp) as carried through BFME 1; the donor's DamageTypeFlags set
/// type does not exist in this body.
/// \see INI::parseDeathTypeFlags, INI::parseVeterancyLevelFlags, INI::scanIndexList

/// \fn static void INI::parseDeathTypeFlags(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers
/// \brief Field parser: reads a list of death type names, written with ALL,
///        NONE, +NAME and -NAME, into a 32-bit mask.
///
/// The shared die-condition table of the die modules reads its DeathTypes
/// field with this parser. DieMuxData::isDieApplicable tests the death type
/// carried by the damage that killed the object, as a shift of that type
/// minus one against this mask, and reports the die module as not applicable
/// when the bit is clear. \ref grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// lists the other conditions in that table.
///
/// \par Syntax
/// The same as INI::parseDamageTypeFlags: tokens apply left to right to a
/// mask that starts with every bit set, ALL and NONE set and clear everything,
/// +NAME and -NAME set and clear one bit, and any other token stops the load.
/// The message here reads "ALL, NONE, +, or - expected", with an extra
/// comma compared with the damage and veterancy parsers. A list that only
/// adds names (`DeathTypes = +BURNED`) leaves every bit set; write
/// `NONE +BURNED` to select.
///
/// \par Names and bits
/// NAME is looked up without regard to case in a table of 24 names: NORMAL,
/// NONE, CRUSHED, BURNED, EXPLODED, POISONED, TOPPLED, FLOODED, SUICIDED,
/// LASERED, DETONATED, SPLATTED, POISONED_BETA, EXTRA_2, EXTRA_3, EXTRA_4,
/// EXTRA_5, EXTRA_6, EXTRA_7, EXTRA_8, KNOCKBACK, SUPERNATURAL, FADED and
/// SLAUGHTERED. Position n sets bit n-1, so NONE is bit 0 and NORMAL, at
/// position 0, is bit 31 (see INI::parseDamageTypeFlags for why). The word
/// NONE is both a keyword and a table name: written bare it clears the
/// mask, and written +NONE or -NONE it sets or clears the bit of the death
/// type of that name, an entry like the others in the table.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The 32-bit mask field.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \note Zero Hour: the same loop with the same first 20 names (NORMAL to
///       EXTRA_8); its 21st name is POISONED_GAMMA, where BFME 2's table has
///       KNOCKBACK, SUPERNATURAL, FADED and SLAUGHTERED. Zero Hour throws
///       without message text on a bad token.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which death types a die module reacts to, from the 24
///   names. A mod cannot add a death type: the table is in the executable,
///   and which names the game code actually produces is not covered here.
/// - Logic-side data (\ref page_modding_rules).
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Facts from BFME 2 evidence: the retail DeathTypes field row references
/// this function; the 24 names and the message text are read from the retail
/// data, and the shift is in the retail body and in the body of
/// DieMuxData::isDieApplicable. The loop is Zero Hour's parseDeathTypeFlags.
/// \see INI::parseDamageTypeFlags, INI::parseVeterancyLevelFlags

/// \fn static void INI::parseVeterancyLevelFlags(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers
/// \brief Field parser: reads a list of veterancy level names, written with
///        ALL, NONE, +NAME and -NAME, into a 32-bit mask.
///
/// Zero Hour uses this parser for the VeterancyLevels field of its shared
/// die-condition table. The BFME 2 die-condition table has no such field,
/// and a search of the retail executable for this function's address finds
/// no field-table row, call or other reference to it. No INI field is
/// therefore known to reach it in BFME 2, and the parser is documented for
/// completeness.
///
/// \par Syntax
/// The same as INI::parseDamageTypeFlags, including the full starting mask
/// and the message "ALL, NONE, + or - expected". The names are the four in
/// TheVeterancyNames, the table the weapon veterancy fields also scan:
/// REGULAR, VETERAN, ELITE and HEROIC. Position n sets bit n-1, so VETERAN
/// is bit 0, ELITE bit 1, HEROIC bit 2 and REGULAR, at position 0, bit 31
/// (see INI::parseDamageTypeFlags).
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The 32-bit mask field.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \warning The name rests on Zero Hour's source order and on this body
///          being a twin of the damage parser over TheVeterancyNames. No
///          retail reference confirms what calls it in BFME 2.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The loop is Zero Hour's parseVeterancyLevelFlags. From BFME 2 evidence:
/// the body, the four names, and the absence of a reference to the function.
/// \see INI::parseDamageTypeFlags, INI::parseDeathTypeFlags

/// \fn static void INI::parseAndTranslateLabel(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers
/// \brief Field parser: reads one string-table label and stores its
///        translated text in a UnicodeString field.
///
/// Fields that show text to the player, such as a science's DisplayName and
/// Description, a rank's RankName and the key-binding names in CommandMap
/// blocks, are written in INI as a label, not as text. This parser asks the
/// text interface (TheGameText) for the label's translation while the field
/// is read and copies the text into the field. The INI file therefore holds
/// no language-specific text, and the field holds the text of the language
/// that was loaded.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The label is the next token (the default separators, so a label
///   cannot contain a space; at least one token is required). Labels have the
///   form CATEGORY:Name, as in the GUI:FullGameName label that
///   GameTextManager::init fetches.
/// - TheGameText->fetch(label) returns the text by value. If that text is
///   empty (no characters), the load stops with a message of the form "Label
///   'NAME' not found in game text", where NAME is the label.
/// - Otherwise the text is copied into the field with the wide string
///   class's set function. Only the translated text is kept, never the
///   label.
///
/// A label that is missing from the tables does not necessarily trip the
/// empty-text test. Zero Hour: fetch returns a placeholder reading MISSING
/// followed by the quoted label, so only a label that exists with empty
/// text fails. BFME 2's GameTextManager::fetchPtr builds the same
/// placeholder when neither the main table nor the map's table has the
/// label. The by-value fetch that this parser calls is not covered here, so
/// the wording of BFME 2's message ("not found") is not verified to describe
/// the case that raises it.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The UnicodeString field that receives a copy of the
///                 translated text.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \note Zero Hour: the same body, except that it throws a generic invalid
///       data error without message text.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which label a field uses, and the text behind it, in the
///   string table (\ref page_modding_strings). To change a name or
///   description either edit the label's text or point the field at another
///   label.
/// - The text is fetched when the field is read, so the string table must
///   be loaded before the INI file. GameEngine::init creates TheGameText
///   before the science store, the rank store and the key-binding map, whose
///   blocks use this parser. A label that only a map's own string file
///   defines is found only when that file has been loaded by then.
/// - Because the text is copied at load time, changing the table afterwards
///   does not change fields already read.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The field is a wide string of 16-bit code units, and the lookup is an
/// indirect call through the text interface, so a replacement text
/// manager only has to provide that fetch. A port that wants run-time
/// language switching cannot rely on these fields: they hold finished text,
/// not labels.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The body is Zero Hour's parseAndTranslateLabel (INI.cpp) through BFME 1.
/// From BFME 2 evidence: the retail field tables that reference this
/// function, the message text, and the order of subsystem creation in
/// GameEngine::init.
/// \see INI::parseGameClientRandomVariable

/// \fn static void INI::parseGameClientRandomVariable(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers
/// \brief Field parser: reads a low value, a high value and an optional
///        distribution name into a GameClientRandomVariable.
///
/// Particle systems and other visual effects state values as ranges that are
/// sampled each time an effect is created, for example a particle system's
/// Radius or the lightning of a cloud effect. This parser fills such a field.
/// The value is sampled later by GameClientRandomVariable::getValue, which
/// draws from the client random stream (\ref grp_w3_random_values), so the
/// result can differ between machines and takes no part in lockstep. The
/// \ref grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers table lists fields that use
/// it.
///
/// \par Syntax
/// `FIELD = low high [distribution]`. Low and high are read with INI::scanReal,
/// so macro names and math expressions that start with \# are accepted
/// (\ref sub_ini_pieces). Both numbers are required. The optional third token
/// is looked up without regard to case, by INI::scanIndexList, in a table of
/// six names: CONSTANT, UNIFORM, GAUSSIAN, TRIANGULAR, LOW_BIAS and HIGH_BIAS.
/// Without it the distribution is UNIFORM. An unknown name stops the load with
/// a message of the form "Token 'NAME' is not a valid member of the index
/// list". Nothing checks that
/// low is not above high.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The three values go to GameClientRandomVariable::setRange, which stores
/// them as written. Any tokens left on the line are ignored by this
/// function.
///
/// \par What each distribution does
/// The parser accepts all six names, but GameClientRandomVariable::getValue
/// handles only two: CONSTANT with equal low and high returns that value,
/// and UNIFORM (and a CONSTANT whose low and high differ) draws a value
/// between low and high. GAUSSIAN, TRIANGULAR, LOW_BIAS and HIGH_BIAS are
/// accepted and then sampled as 0. Zero Hour behaves the same way.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The GameClientRandomVariable to fill.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's INI.h declares only this client form. This page does
///       not establish whether BFME 2 has a matching parser for the logic
///       stream, so a logic range field is not documented here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: the range and the distribution of every field in the
///   table. Use CONSTANT with equal values, or UNIFORM, and avoid the other
///   four names, which yield 0.
/// - The sampling is cosmetic, but the file that holds the field may still be
///   covered by the INI CRC (\ref sub_ini_modding says which loads reach the
///   CRC writer and what is not established about it), so players joining a
///   game together should use the same files.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Values are single-precision floats read in the x87 mode that every
/// INI load sets (\ref sub_ini_remaster). Visual effect ranges are the
/// data a remaster most plausibly retunes, and since the client stream is
/// separate, changing them does not touch logic.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The body is Zero Hour's parseGameClientRandomVariable (INI.cpp). From
/// BFME 2 evidence: the retail field tables that reference it, the six
/// distribution names, and getValue's handling of the types.
/// \see INI::parseAndTranslateLabel, \ref grp_w3_random_values, \ref grp_w3_fx_particle_modules

/// \fn static void INI::parseDrawGroupNumberDefinition(INI *ini)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers
/// \brief Block parser for DrawGroupInfo: sets how the control-group number
///        over a unit is drawn.
///
/// A unit that belongs to a control group shows the group's number, 0 to 9,
/// next to a screen rectangle that its drawable keeps. There is one DrawGroupInfo record
/// (TheDrawGroupInfo), so the block takes no name: the field lines follow
/// the keyword directly. GameClient::GameClient creates the record with
/// defaults and GameClient::init loads Data\\INI\\DrawGroupInfo.ini into
/// it with INI_LOAD_OVERWRITE and no CRC writer.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// If TheDrawGroupInfo is null, the load stops with "TheDrawGroupInfo==NULL".
/// Otherwise the block is read with INI::initFromINI against the record's
/// field table. Fields (BFME 2's table has these twelve, as Zero Hour's does):
/// | Field | Value | Meaning |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | UsePlayerColor | bool | draw the number in the owning player's colour; when true ColorForText is not used |
/// | ColorForText, ColorForTextDropShadow | colour | the number's colour and its shadow colour, read by INI::parseColorInt |
/// | FontName, FontSize, FontIsBold | quoted string, int, bool | the numeral font |
/// | DropShadowOffsetX, DropShadowOffsetY | int | shadow offset passed to the display string's draw |
/// | DrawPositionXPixel, DrawPositionYPixel | int | offset from the anchor in pixels (see parseInt) |
/// | DrawPositionXPercent, DrawPositionYPercent | percentage | offset as a fraction of the anchor rectangle (see parsePercentToReal) |
///
/// After the file is loaded, GameClient::init replaces the three font fields
/// with the Language definition's DrawGroupInfoFont (name, size and bold flag)
/// whenever that font name is not empty. For a language that sets it, the
/// FontName, FontSize and FontIsBold lines of this block have no lasting
/// effect.
///
/// The record's constructor sets, per the field table's layout, the Arial
/// font at size 12, not bold, the player colour, a white text colour, an
/// opaque black shadow, a shadow offset of -1 and -1, and offsets of zero
/// (X as a percentage, Y in pixels). A field the file omits keeps that
/// value.
///
/// \par How the record is used
/// W3DDisplayStringManager::postProcessLoad builds the group numeral strings
/// with the record's font and the label NUMBER:n for each number. The
/// drawable code that draws a unit's group number reads the colours and the
/// offsets: it takes the drawable's anchor rectangle, adds the pixel offset or
/// the percentage of the rectangle's size, moves the text left and up by its
/// own extents, and draws it with the shadow offsets. It draws nothing when
/// the unit's group number is outside 0 to 9.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the DrawGroupInfo keyword.
///
/// \note Zero Hour: the same block and the same twelve fields. Zero Hour's
///       record starts with a font size of 10 and offsets of -5 percent
///       (X) and -10 pixels (Y); BFME 2's starts with 12 and zero. A null
///       record throws without message text there.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: the colours, shadow, font (unless the language overrides
///   it) and position of the number, in DrawGroupInfo.ini. The file is
///   loaded by a fixed path (\ref sub_ini_entry) without the CRC writer.
///   Unlike most block files, the number's look is purely visual.
/// - Hard-coded: the group numbers (0 to 9), the NUMBER:n labels that give
///   the digit text, and the rule that the text is drawn up and to the left
///   of the offset point.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Pixel offsets and the shadow offsets are raw screen pixels added without
/// scaling, so they do not follow resolution. The percentage form scales
/// with the anchor rectangle and is the one to use for a resolution-independent
/// layout. The font size goes to the font library as a size value, and its
/// relation to screen size is the font library's.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The parser and table are Zero Hour's INIDrawGroupInfo.cpp through BFME 1.
/// From BFME 2 evidence: the block keyword, the twelve table rows and their
/// offsets, the null-record message, the load in GameClient::init and the
/// language override, the constructor's stores, and the uses described under
/// "How the record is used". The meaning of constructor fields is taken from
/// the table offsets, not from a named constructor.
/// \see parseInt, parsePercentToReal

/// \fn void parseInt(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for DrawPositionXPixel and DrawPositionYPixel: stores
///        a pixel offset in the DrawGroupInfo record.
///
/// This is a free function in INIDrawGroupInfo.cpp (the ledger's name for it
/// has no INI:: prefix) and is not INI::parseInt, which it calls. Zero Hour
/// has the same two helper functions at file scope.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The field table row has offset 0 and the userData 0 for X or 1 for Y, so
/// store is the DrawGroupInfo record itself. The function picks the record's
/// X or Y offset slot by userData, sets the record's "X (or Y) offset is in
/// pixels" flag, then reads an integer into the slot with INI::parseInt. The
/// pixel and percentage forms share one slot (a union of an integer and a
/// real), and the flag says which one the slot now holds. Whichever of the
/// pixel and percentage lines is read last decides, including across
/// files.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [in,out] The DrawGroupInfo record (not a field address).
/// \param userData Null selects the X offset; any other value (the table uses
///                 1) selects the Y offset.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Because the value is an integer in raw pixels, it is not scaled to the
/// window size. Use the percentage fields when the same file must serve
/// several resolutions.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Zero Hour's function of the same name. In BFME 2 the table rows and the
/// userData values are read from the retail field table.
/// \see parsePercentToReal, INI::parseDrawGroupNumberDefinition

/// \fn void parsePercentToReal(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for DrawPositionXPercent and DrawPositionYPercent:
///        stores a percentage offset in the DrawGroupInfo record.
///
/// A free function in INIDrawGroupInfo.cpp (the ledger's name for it has no
/// INI:: prefix), not INI::parsePercentToReal, which it calls.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The same selection as parseInt: userData 0 picks the X slot and any other
/// value the Y slot, and the "offset is in pixels" flag is cleared. The value
/// is then read with INI::parsePercentToReal. That function splits the token
/// at the percent separator set, which includes the percent sign, so -5\%
/// and -5 read the same, and stores the number scaled by 0.01, so -5 is
/// stored as -0.05. The drawing code multiplies the stored fraction by the
/// anchor rectangle's width or height.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [in,out] The DrawGroupInfo record (not a field address).
/// \param userData Null selects the X offset; any other value (the table uses
///                 1) selects the Y offset.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Use this form for a position that must follow the size of the area it is
/// anchored to. A later pixel line for the same axis replaces it.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Zero Hour's function of the same name. The scaling by 0.01 and the percent
/// separators are read from BFME 2's INI::parsePercentToReal and the INI
/// constructor.
/// \see parseInt, INI::parseDrawGroupNumberDefinition

/// \fn static void INI::parseTerrainDefinition(INI *ini)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_ini_flag_label_random_parsers
/// \brief Block parser for Terrain: finds or creates one terrain type by name
///        and reads its fields.
///
/// A terrain type names a ground texture and a few properties of it. The
/// blocks fill TheTerrainTypes, the terrain type collection that
/// GameEngine::init creates through SubsystemInterfaceList::initSubsystem
/// with the CRC writer. A map's texture classes refer to terrain types by
/// name: the map loader's texture-class reader (WorldHeightMap::readTexClass)
/// resolves the class name through TerrainTypeCollection::findTerrain and
/// skips the class when no terrain type has that name. The folder the
/// texture file is opened from is not established here for BFME 2. Zero Hour:
/// the same reader joins the type's Texture name to its terrain texture
/// directory, Art/Terrain/ (defined in its FileSystem.h); BFME 2's reader is
/// expected to use the same folder, which is not independently established
/// here.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The name is the next token after the Terrain keyword.
/// - TerrainTypeCollection::findTerrain walks the collection's list and
///   returns the type whose name compares equal. The comparison is
///   AsciiString::compare, which is case-sensitive
///   (\ref grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search).
/// - If there is none, TerrainTypeCollection::newTerrain creates one. It
///   starts as a copy of the type named DefaultTerrain when one exists, gets
///   the name, and is linked in at the head of the list.
/// - The fields are read with INI::initFromINI into the type, so a second
///   block with the same name edits the existing type and keeps the fields
///   it does not state. The result of newTerrain is not tested for null.
///
/// \par Fields
/// | Field | Value |
/// |---|---|
/// | Texture | the texture file name, read by INI::parseAsciiString |
/// | BlendEdges | bool; Zero Hour: the terrain contains custom blend edges |
/// | Class | one or more class names (see below) |
/// | RestrictConstruction | bool; Zero Hour: construction is not allowed on this terrain (its BuildAssistant tests it) |
/// | TerrainObject | a name followed by a real number; each TerrainObject line appends one such pair to a list in the type |
///
/// Class is a list: BFME 2 stores each token, resolved to its position in the
/// class name table, in a growable list, and the word None (any case) empties
/// the list and ends the parser's reading of that line. Each Class line first
/// empties the list, then adds the names on that line, so a later Class line,
/// or a Class line in a block that edits an existing type, replaces the
/// earlier classes rather than adding to them. A new type that started as a
/// copy of DefaultTerrain gets that type's class list copied with it, and
/// its own first Class line replaces that list the same way. The table has 47 names: NONE, NEXT, Region, Minas_Tirith,
/// Osgiliath, Dead_Marshes, Edoras, Helm's_Deep, Paths_of_Dead,
/// Fangorn_Forest, Misty_Mountains, Rivendell, Moria, The_Shire, Isengard,
/// Mordor, Misc_Village, Misc_Grassland, Misc_Town, Misc_Woodland,
/// Misc_Water, Type, Grass, Rock, Dirt, Snow, Cliff, Misc, DESERT_1,
/// DESERT_2, DESERT_3, EASTERN_EUROPE_1, EASTERN_EUROPE_2,
/// EASTERN_EUROPE_3, SWISS_1, SWISS_2, SWISS_3, SNOW_1, SNOW_2, SNOW_3,
/// ASPHALT, CONCRETE, TRANSITION, SAND, WOOD, BLEND_EDGE and LOTR_NEW. Names
/// are matched without regard to case, an unknown name stops the load, and
/// because None is tested first the table's own NONE entry cannot be selected
/// through this parser. What reads the class list and the TerrainObject list
/// is not established here.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the Terrain keyword.
///
/// \note Zero Hour: the same function, but its Class field is a single value
///       read by INI::parseIndexList from a shorter name table (NONE, DESERT_1
///       and so on), and it has no TerrainObject field. A failed allocation
///       is only a debug assertion there.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: the terrain type names, their textures and flags. Map
///   texture classes must use these names exactly, with the same case.
///   Defining `Terrain DefaultTerrain` first gives every later terrain type
///   the same starting values. A Class line does not add to an inherited
///   list, it replaces it (see Fields).
/// - Hard-coded: the five field names and the class name table. How the
///   Texture name becomes a file path is decided by the map loader, not by
///   this parser (see above for what is and is not established about it).
/// - The collection is created with the CRC writer passed to
///   SubsystemInterfaceList::initSubsystem, so its files are very likely
///   covered by the INI CRC that join requests carry. Zero Hour: that CRC
///   covers the text of each line read through the writer; what BFME 2's
///   matching call hashes is not established here (\ref sub_ini_modding).
///   Which file holds the blocks is decided by the subsystem legend entry
///   for TheTerrainTypes, not by this function.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The function is Zero Hour's INI::parseTerrainDefinition. From BFME 2
/// evidence: the Terrain keyword's registration, the field table (five rows)
/// and the class name table in the retail data, and the bodies of findTerrain,
/// newTerrain, the type's copy assignment (which copies the class list) and
/// the Class field's list parser (which empties the list before reading any
/// token). The texture-class reader's lookup through findTerrain and its skip
/// when no terrain type has the name are from BFME 2's body of that reader.
/// The Art/Terrain/ folder is Zero Hour's (its FileSystem.h and
/// WorldHeightMap.cpp); BFME 2's folder is not established. The DefaultTerrain
/// copy is in BFME 2's newTerrain; the meanings of BlendEdges and
/// RestrictConstruction are Zero Hour's.
/// \see INI::initFromINI

/// \fn void iniParseWaterTexturesDefinition(INI *ini)
/// \brief Block parser for WaterTextureList: appends texture names to one of
///        six named water texture lists.
///
/// The block names a list, such as RiverTextures, and its Texture lines add
/// texture file names to it. Zero Hour has no block of this keyword; BFME 1
/// has one.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The name is the next token after the keyword.
/// - The lookup searches a fixed table of six named lists, comparing names
///   without regard to case. The six names, from the table initialiser in the
///   executable, are RiverTextures, WaterBumpMapTextures,
///   RiverAlphaEdgeTextures, WaterSkyTextures, RiverSparkleTextures and
///   RiverNoiseTextures. (The initialiser's caller is not established, but it
///   fills the same layout the lookup walks.) The table is reached through a
///   global pointer that this parser reads; the parser does not test it for
///   null, and a search of the retail image for that global's address finds
///   only this parser's read, so what fills it is not established.
/// - If no list has the name, the function writes the debug report "Unable
///   to find water texture type 'NAME'" through the engine's debug-report
///   path and returns without reading the block's field lines (whether that
///   report also stops the game in retail is not established here). As with
///   other skipped block parsers (\ref grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers),
///   the loader then reads the next line as a new top-level line, which
///   would normally end the load with an unknown block error.
/// - Otherwise the block is read with INI::initFromINI against a one-row
///   table whose only field is Texture. It is read by
///   INI::parseAsciiStringVectorAppend: every remaining token of the line
///   is appended to the list, a macro that expands to several words
///   contributes several entries, and the list is never cleared, so more
///   Texture lines (and later files) add more entries.
/// - Finally the list is dumped: one line per texture, formatted as the
///   list's name, a space and the texture, goes to OutputDebugStringA. The
///   call is not conditional, so the debugger sees it in a retail build as
///   well.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the WaterTextureList keyword.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: the texture names in each of the six lists.
/// - Hard-coded: the six list names, the single Texture field, and the
///   debug output. What reads the lists at run time is not established here
///   (water drawing is described in \ref sub_w3d_rendering).
/// - Which file holds these blocks, and whether its load feeds the INI CRC,
///   is not established here (\ref sub_ini_modding explains which loads do).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The only platform call is the Win32 OutputDebugStringA used by the list
/// dump; it can be replaced by any logging sink.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The function follows BFME 1's INIWaterTextureList.cpp, adapted to BFME 2's
/// shared string class and debug-report ABI. From BFME 2 evidence: the
/// keyword's registration, the one-row table, the six names and the dump
/// format. The function name is the project's, not an export.
/// \see INI::parseTerrainDefinition
